-- title:
--   Index of specialty equals ℓ(W-D) at a Weil differential
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, and assume `HasPrincipalDivisors K F`: every nonzero $f \in F$ admits a divisor $D$ on the set of places `Place K F` with $D(v) = v.\mathrm{ord}(f)$ at every place and $\deg D = 0$. Assume `WeilDifferentialRankOne K F`: for every nonzero element $\varphi$ of the Weil differential module $\bigsqcup_E \Omega(E)$ — the supremum over divisors $E$ of the annihilators $\Omega(E)$ in the $K$-dual of the adele space of the submodule generated by the $E$-bounded adeles together with the image of the diagonal map $F \to \prod_v F$ — and every $\mu$ in that module, there is a unique $f \in F$ with $\mu = f \cdot \varphi$, the action being the dual of multiplication by $f$ on the adele space. Let $W$ be a divisor and $\varphi$ a $K$-linear functional on the adele space with $\varphi \in \Omega(W)$, $\varphi \neq 0$, and $W$ maximal for this: every divisor $E$ with $\varphi \in \Omega(E)$ satisfies $E \le W$. Then for every divisor $D$ the index of specialty of $D$, namely the $K$-dimension of the quotient of the adele space by the sum of the $D$-bounded adeles and the diagonal copy of $F$, equals $\ell(W - D) = \dim_K L(W-D)$.
--
--   This is the Weil–Serre duality step of the adelic proof of the Riemann–Roch theorem, expressed in terms of dimensions: the index of specialty $i(D)$ is computed as $\ell(W-D)$ for $W$ the divisor of a nonzero Weil differential. It feeds into [`AlgebraicCurve.exists_weilCanonical_riemannRoch`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.exists_weilCanonical_riemannRoch), where $W$ plays the role of a canonical divisor and the identity $\ell(D) - \ell(W-D)$ takes its classical form.
